  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1143 AM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe current forecast has a little bit for everyone. For those who\nlike the summer-time weather, you'll enjoy the first part of the \nforecast. For those who are looking forward to fall-like weather, \nyou will like the second part of the forecast.\n\nFriday and Saturday will continue the sunny skies and warm, well\nabove normal high temps. Expected highs are in the 80s to low 90s\nwith gusty southwest winds. Elevated to critical fire weather \nconditions are likely through Saturday due to low RH and gusty \nwinds, more information can be found in the FIRE WEATHER section \nbelow.\n\nAfter Saturday, a change in the weather pattern will happen. Upper\nlevel winds will swing to the west/southwest as a trough of low\npressure deepens over the Pacific northwest. And that low is\nprojected to track southward along the coast before kicking\neastward toward the the central US. That pattern change will bring\nmuch cooler weather to the area, high temps will drop to the 50s \nand 60s and low temps will drop to the 30s and 40s.\n\nThis change will also bring back the rain chances. The first \nround will begin to move into the far western CWA zones late \nSaturday with PoPS lingering into Monday night for the entire CWA.\nIf the previously mentioned low's track doesn't change, then a \nbreak in the rain is likely Tuesday before another round of rain \nmoves into the area from the southwest Tuesday night with chances \nlasting through next Thursday. While model ensembles are still \ntrying to hint at the snow potential with each round of precip, \nthe bulk of the model evidence shows that rain is the more likely \nprecip type.",
